WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
I've got tons of videos 
on type 2 diabetes,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.650
the lifestyle disease,

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.500
but can a plant-based diet 
have any impact on type 1 diabetes,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.722
the autoimmune disease?

00:00:11.722 --> 00:00:13.999
Watch the video to find out.

00:00:14.231 --> 00:00:24.136
"Treating Type 1 Diabetes
with a Plant-Based Diet"

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.980
The brand-new International Journal
of Disease Reversal and Prevention

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.840
had its share of typical plant-
based miraculous disease reversals.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.060
After having not one but two
heart attacks within two months,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.480
a whole food, plant-based diet
and no more chest pain,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.000
controlling his cholesterol,
blood pressure, and blood sugars

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.900
while losing 50 pounds as
a neat little side-bonus.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.000
Yet the numbers don’t capture the
transformation, the resurrection

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.380
from feeling like a “dead man walking”
to getting his life back.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.280
I already discussed the cases of
autoimmune inflammatory

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.140
disease reversal, the psoriasis, the lupus,
nephritis (kidney inflammation);

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.040
and speaking of autoimmune-
diseases-we-didn’t-think-

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.580
we-could-do-anything-
about, type 1 diabetes.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.900
In contrast to type 2 diabetes,
which is a lifestyle disease

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.020
that can be prevented and reversed
with a healthy enough diet and lifestyle,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.400
type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune
disease in which your body attacks

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.520
your own pancreas, killing off
your insulin-producing cells

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.060
condemning you to a
life of insulin injections—

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.660
unless, perhaps, you
catch it early enough.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.220
Maybe if we can switch people early
enough to a healthy enough diet,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.140
we can reverse the course by
blunting that autoimmune inflammation.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.800
Now, we know patients with type 1
may be able to reduce

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.300
insulin requirements and achieve better
blood sugar control with healthier diets.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.900
For example, randomize children
and teens to a nutritional intervention

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.320
in which they boost the whole
plant food density of their diet,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.540
meaning eating more whole grains,
whole fruit, vegetables, legumes

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.000
(which are beans, split peas, chickpeas,
and lentils), nuts, and seeds.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.740
And the more whole plant foods,
the better the blood sugar control.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.700
The fact that more whole
fruits was associated with better

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.400
blood sugar control has
important clinical implications

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.940
for nutrition education in
those struggling with type 1.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.900
We should be educating them
on the beneﬁts of fruit intake

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.100
and allaying “erroneous concerns that
fruit may adversely affect blood sugar".

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.860
But this case series went beyond just
proposing better control of the symptom

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.660
of diabetes—high blood sugars—but
better control of the disease itself,

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.320
suggesting the anti-inﬂammatory
eﬀects of whole healthy plant foods

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.700
may slow or prevent further destruction
of the insulin-producing cells

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.240
of the pancreas, if the dietary
intervention is initiated early enough.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.720
Where are they getting this
from? Check it out.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.880
One patient who began a vegetable-
rich diet at age three immediately

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.140
following diagnosis of type 1
diabetes, but three years later

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.160
still has yet to require insulin
therapy, while experiencing

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.980
a steady decline in autoantibody levels,
markers of insulin cell destruction.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.480
Another child, who didn’t start
eating healthier until several months

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.560
after diagnosis, maintains a
low dose of insulin with good control.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.280
And even if the insulin-producing
cells have been utterly destroyed,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.480
type 1 diabetics can still enjoy
dramatically reduced insulin requirements

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.160
and reduced inflammation and
reduced cardiovascular risk,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.320
which is the #1 cause of death for
type 1 diabetics over the age of 30.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.580
Type 1’s have 11 to 14 times the risk
of death from cardiovascular disease

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.180
compared to the general population,
and it’s already the #1 killer

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.600
among the public, so it’s like
11 to 14 times more important

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.140
for type 1 diabetics to be on
the only diet and lifestyle program

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.100
ever proven to reverse heart
disease in the majority of patients—

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.460
one centered around whole plant foods.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.540
And the fact that it may also
help control the disease itself

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.600
is just sugar-free icing on the cake.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.020
All this exciting new research was
just from the first issue of the journal!

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:26.040
As a bonus there’s a companion
publication called the Disease Reversal

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.160
and Prevention Digest,
a companion publication to the

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.260
International Journal of
Disease Reversal and Prevention

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.880
for the lay public with the
belief I wholeheartedly share

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.600
that everyone has a right
to understand the science

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.980
that could impact their health.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.760
And so, you can go behind the
scenes and hear directly from

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:49.380
the author of the lupus series,
with bonus interviews from luminaries

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.860
like Dean Ornish, practical
tips from dietitians on making

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.400
the transition towards a healthier
diet, complete with recipes.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.260
The second issue continued
to feature practical tips

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:05.580
like how to eat plant-based on
a budget, what Dr. Klaper is doing

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.960
to educate medical students, what
Audrey Sanchez from Balanced is doing

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.140
to help change school lunches,
and if you think that’s hard,

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.420
Dr. Ostfeld got healthy foods
served in a hospital. What a concept!

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.200
And, what magazine would be
complete without an article

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.240
to improve your sex life.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:29.340
Now, while the journal itself is
free, downloadable at IJDRP.org,

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.900
the companion digest, available
at diseasereversaldigest.com,

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.358
carries a subscription fee, but I,
for one, am a proud subscriber.

